#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161799 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7076c0-fa73-5cb1-9206-2b129dd08be1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "79fbc4ff-042e-5454-9fa4-f2582c10a51a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9132" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/26f1037a6da42fdd87ef588234019d23d555dba8d9c02d5469215b2445da8e45.png)\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1,$ and $\\lambda_3=3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda=-1{:}$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(-1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(-1) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(-1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=-1.$\n\nComputing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1,$ say, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0} \\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/c59f5929bdbe3b35e42dc30266ac9ca947c3536e1c019b708a2d48170ea8222b.png)\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161800 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce5e23-e0ef-5f59-afe8-5c63d901b290" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "b281dc8a-0cef-50c5-96f4-b61dbcea27f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/77995d8d2d70bc6eeb059663883375dbcad8820df3bafc225f854367ee122977.png)\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3 \\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda=2{:}$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3 \\times 3$ Jordan block $J_3(2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ corresponding to $\\lambda=2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_3 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of one $2 \\times 2$ and one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block, say $J_2(2) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ corresponding to $\\lambda=2$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=2.$\n\nComputing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-2I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2,$ say, $\\mathbf{v}_1.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_3 \\xrightarrow{A-2 I} \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-2 I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-2 I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/3fba5b4e0ccc48a2d122b457509e744265b2aa5cfd3625808ebb12fcd421be56.png) \n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161801 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "1151303f-2da6-5587-beda-69ff592a5ca3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9134" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/9e6933df2cebe6522a9f7981a2b4646fa830abcbe68659411f12c41953eb9759.png)\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=8,$ $\\lambda_2=7,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/79bf6b6c00e76745d278dcc75e8105447d31074ad42f998f684e66b3929178aa.png)\n\nA Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks. So, another Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073225/assets/cf60314522736fab996889e13f914f466bff1a91f6cc8c0b43a7d8635ff48a70.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b35a216b-645a-5866-9bad-8c82b805a8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158839} {:answer/id #uuid "b98f01f7-c21e-5756-9fde-8a6537a40dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158840} {:answer/id #uuid "02f31b4c-ec80-5be4-96c9-cb0cb51a9c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158841} {:answer/id #uuid "3679e66f-a526-5f6b-b617-ad2da7319af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158842} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3a13d5-dc56-5c06-8677-153e8913590d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e3a13d5-dc56-5c06-8677-153e8913590d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158843} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a9db42-24b2-55a7-899b-dfed4b330dd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "132107ab-2199-5d46-b51c-37977a6efa1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109390" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7076c0-fa73-5cb1-9206-2b129dd08be1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbc64cd6-c4c1-5218-bab6-01676d7df07b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158851} {:answer/id #uuid "e599ef31-9f85-5982-98ff-b7aeaf59e29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158852} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f9d8c-4754-5543-82c8-d7b1ebb985a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158853} {:answer/id #uuid "31b8fe9e-bdf1-56d2-838c-f1847fc3f684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158854} {:answer/id #uuid "7b75c4c0-f0b3-532e-8858-ecd06f7acef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc64cd6-c4c1-5218-bab6-01676d7df07b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158851} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c8bea1-9a8d-5145-99e8-74fc05a07d2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0e31f06-6711-501a-84f6-2cd9da1c46e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109395" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(4)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =4.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-2:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nComputing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce5e23-e0ef-5f59-afe8-5c63d901b290" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de432ad9-c2fc-5cba-a916-52456fc06cd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158857} {:answer/id #uuid "2191cfbc-2b25-50c5-8a08-f9763889a765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158858} {:answer/id #uuid "373c92bb-30d1-5fb5-b767-581a3095b7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158859} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba65257-15b4-5c0f-a49a-52bf70c2dd79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158860} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c51cf-d4cd-55d6-8ef0-e0294e820ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c51cf-d4cd-55d6-8ef0-e0294e820ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158861} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c76e11-f840-5fb2-ac0a-f2403a5b2a90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eea9bca5-e2af-5064-b65d-73888b1b186f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109397" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3\\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-3:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block ${J}_{3}(-3).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of one $2\\times 2$ and one $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, say ${J}_{2}(-3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nComputing $A+3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+3I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f26b028e-e0a9-5c43-8533-a9026eb2f001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378422} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e9da5c-dc13-52c6-9097-9f838f3908e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378423} {:answer/id #uuid "64a21075-f6d3-5543-81c8-be9a89c0eb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378424} {:answer/id #uuid "70e62e33-04d0-543b-b2ad-0ccabcdfdc8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f8a196-f6bb-534d-abf3-044db4b399a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9f8a196-f6bb-534d-abf3-044db4b399a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378426} :answer-field/id #uuid "1952d621-3146-52cc-9263-6371aabdd0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "260c52c2-ca65-5b6e-8e1a-cb3ad0622374" :question/math-academy-id "q-110375" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -9 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9342213-d44b-505e-921e-f560c49f9f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378429} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c4adb-2492-5eec-a608-e08181b068ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378430} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdf588e-0d2d-5d02-9706-018d1ad51ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378431} {:answer/id #uuid "ae20f2dd-f030-5f5f-a427-488283d2b3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378432} {:answer/id #uuid "91875b79-26da-5385-8c78-cb485860095b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f52c4adb-2492-5eec-a608-e08181b068ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378430} :answer-field/id #uuid "4959b31e-7716-5254-9f9d-676fde7a93f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3167bd6-6991-5e5c-9ec0-cd2757f2b8e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110377" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 9 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(8)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378434 :knowledge-point/_questions 